  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "wave-trap-on-real-data",
      "title": "Initialize precedes PoolCreated, and runs in the second wave, for every pool",
      "statement": "data_source_creations records all thirteen pools created in this window, each with the handlers of its creating transaction and its own handlers in that block. Every one was created inside a position-manager multicall, which is why position-manager handlers share the row. In every row the Factory's handlePoolCreated at some log L and the position manager's handleTransfer and handleIncreaseLiquidity, a few logs later, run in wave 0, while the new pool's handleInitialize at log L-1 and its handleMint, which sits between PoolCreated and the position manager's Transfer, run in wave 1 (13,899,663: wave 0 runs logs 172, 177 and 178, then wave 1 runs 171 and 176). The Pool data source does not exist until handlePoolCreated runs, so Graph Node rescans the block for it in a second wave; max_waves is 2, and every handleInitialize in the window is one of these thirteen. An extractor that orders by log index alone would run handleInitialize before handlePoolCreated and handleMint before handleIncreaseLiquidity, and both are wrong."
    },
    {
      "id": "root-chain-and-determinism",
      "title": "Replayed from the root checkpoint with recording off, the window is byte-identical",
      "statement": "The same 20,000 blocks were replayed a second time, continuing with --checkpoint-in from the recording root-prefix checkpoint (boundary 13,899,662, whose hash is block 13,899,663's parent hash), with the finished cassette and recording off, so any call the first run had not recorded would have failed. The second run's BCF1 stream (d5ab1d90…a59e) and final store (8318a834…d8b0) are byte-identical to this run's. Recording and replay are therefore indistinguishable in output, and 13,843,704 to 13,919,662 is one root-anchored chain of frames and PoI digests."
    },
    {
      "id": "throughput-and-memory",
      "title": "Recording is RPC-bound; replay costs about 31 ms of CPU per handler group",
      "statement": "This recording run took 997.0 s (20.1 blocks/s, maximum resident set 586 MB). It started at 80 blocks/s and fell to 7 to 15 blocks/s once density reached 0.4 to 1.1 handler groups per block, where it executed 6 to 10 groups/s: each group makes about 1.5 eth_calls, paced 40 ms apart, so the RPC is the bottleneck. The replay with the warm cassette took 462.6 s (43.2 blocks/s, 267 MB, 258.7 s user CPU) and ran 20 to 30 groups/s in the densest stretches. Subtracting the root prefix's 3.4 ms of CPU per block leaves about 31 ms of CPU per handler group, frame sealing included. At the roughly 58 groups per block measured near head that is about 1.8 CPU-seconds per block, about 0.5 blocks/s, an estimate that will only rise with the store: the deployed mapping is an oracle for windows, not a route for the whole range."
    },
    {
      "id": "handlers-and-calls",
      "title": "Every handler but Flash ran, cleanly, against block-hash-pinned calls",
      "statement": "6,089 handler invocations, every one ok: handleSwap 3,014, handleTransfer 941, handleBurn 536, handleCollect 536, handleIncreaseLiquidity 475, handleMint 475, handleDecreaseLiquidity 86, handlePoolCreated 13 and handleInitialize 13. No deterministic error, so no BigDecimal outside the BlockChanges v1 precision contract reached a frame. Collect, Flash and TickHourData were never written. The cassette's 9,394 rows cover nine function signatures, every call made with EIP-1898 {blockHash, requireCanonical} against base.rpc.pinax.network; its 46 reverts are all NonfungiblePositionManager.positions on a token the same block burns (a Transfer to the zero address, checked with eth_getLogs for all 46): the call reads end-of-block state, so a handler running before the burn in log order still sees the revert. The declared calls in the manifest are resolved and attached by graph-node's own match_and_decode; Graph Node executes them only to warm its call cache, so the handlers' own ethereum.call is what the cassette answers."
    }
  ]
